fnerd:
> I can think of a couple examples (extra cortex layer, more 
> vision-processing maps) where humans have more hard wiring
> than other animals, but not the reverse.  I would agree humans 
> are somehow more plastic and less independent at birth, but not 
> necessarily that that's due to less hard wiring.

There is indirect evidence (from other animals!) that some of the wiring
at birth is not hard-wiring but has evolved, neural net style in the
womb.  That's not to say that there isn't hard wiring in some sense,
just that the way this is expressed or emerges is not clear.  I suspect
that our genetic "hard wiring" is expressed in our basic drives like
hunger, thirst, sex, curiosity.  How we break these primal goals into
sub-goals is what we learn, although I accept Robin's point that there
is probably some hard wiring relating to grammars.

