From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Date: Thu, 21 Jul 94 11:12:11 GMT
Subject: [#94-7-177] If Knowledge Limits Learning

Both Robin Hanson and fnerd have raised the issue of innate grammar
structures in our brains (the existence of which I accept) as a problem
for neural nets.  Would we have to load in similar predispositions to
grammar syntaxes into our artificial networks?  I don't think we would
and here's why.

Chimps have been taught to interpret symbols and, in a film clip I saw,
spoken instructions relayed though earphones to remove trainer body
cues.  Whilst the instructions were reasonably simple (like take the bin
outside the room) it seems that chimps indeed *can* pick up the
rudiments of written and oral language - although their larynxes
preclude vocalisation.  Chimps and humans diverged about 7 million years
ago, a long time before spoken or written language appeared in our
ancestors, so far as we know.  If so then we may reasonably conclude
that chimps have no innate grammar structures.

It is true that the chimps have problems with the syntax, but they also
have very limited vocabularies, so I think that the problem lies with
their inferior cranial capacity rather than the syntax per se.  I
speculate that with larger brains chimps could be taught to read and
write as well as humans, and similarly that artificial networks can
acquire language with out any "pre-natal" hard-wiring.

