From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Date: Thu, 21 Jul 94 08:07:54 GMT
Subject: [#94-7-178] If Knowledge Limits Learning 

Peter McCluskey raises lots of points which I don't have answers to:
> there is also a need to prevent unrelated concepts from interfering
> with each other. If you try to train one big backprop net, it will
> often spread the information it uses to solve a task over much of the
> hidden layer, when we need to focus training on a small number of new
> connections to handle each new idea.

I am only using backpropagation as a metaphor and I'm guessing that some
of its generic features will copy over.  Aren't there other algorithms
that localise concepts more?  If the final algorithm includes something
that forms connections between nodes with correlated firing and, at the
same time, merges neurons will similar external connections then this
desired concept clustering might happen emergently.  (As an ignorant
aside, what are the disadvantages with the network having delocalised
representations?)

>  This probably bears some resemblence to a good solution, but I don't
> think instability in weights is a good criterion [for adding new
> nodes]. With properly scaled inputs and a sophisticated training
> algorithm (better than plain backprop), my experience indicates that
> weights usually settle down.

If the hidden layer has too few neurons, will the weights settle down? 
If the network can't form adequate internal representations due to lack
of complexity then it seems to me that it will continue change, although
not in an immediately productive fashion.  An algorithm which inserts
neurons/nodes into dynamic regions might be just what the doctor
ordered, allowing more complex representations.

> I am more concerned about the network learning noise

There may be no easy answer to this, since this is a problem we suffer
from as well.  Look at the synchronicity believers, for example.  Or is
this a reference to over/rotelearning?

> or settling into an overly simplistic solution.

A overly simplistic solution should generate error signals and lead to
network growth, allowing more routes out of a minimum by increasing the
dimension of the weight space.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

