From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Thu, 21 Jul 1994 14:51:44 -0400
Subject: [#94-7-181] If Knowledge Limits Learning 

 price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-7-178:
>I am only using backpropagation as a metaphor and I'm guessing that some
>of its generic features will copy over.  Aren't there other algorithms
>that localise concepts more?  If the final algorithm includes something
>that forms connections between nodes with correlated firing and, at the
>same time, merges neurons will similar external connections then this
>desired concept clustering might happen emergently.  (As an ignorant
>aside, what are the disadvantages with the network having delocalised
>representations?)

 Cascade correlation improves on backprop by adding hidden nodes one at
a time. It still suffers from problems associated with connecting new
nodes to all inputs, outputs and existing hidden nodes.
 There are no real disadvantages to delocalised representations unless
representations for different ideas overlap, in which case learning the
second idea introduces some noise into the first idea. Humans probably
experience a small amount of this as evidenced by the deterioration of
memory with time.
 
>If the hidden layer has too few neurons, will the weights settle down? 

 Changes in existing weights will normally diminish with training even
if there are too few neurons. Cascade correlation will keep adding nodes
until some externally specified criterion is met.

>> I am more concerned about the network learning noise
>
>There may be no easy answer to this, since this is a problem we suffer
>from as well.  Look at the synchronicity believers, for example.  Or is
>this a reference to over/rotelearning?

 I think over/rotelearning is the same problem. If there were an easy
answer then I wouldn't be concerned.
 To quote something you said last year:
>out-moded Popperian view that a new theory must make a falsifiable
>_prediction_.  Weinberg argues that logically and historically this is
>not and has not been the case.  Theories are judged by their simplicity
>and fitness with _pre-existing_ data.  Only later, usually after partial
                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  I think that this pre-existing data is part of what Lenat and Robin
think is missing from your tabula rasa model. I think humans have a way of
balancing simplicity and consistency with existing concepts that has not
yet been captured in a learning algorithm.

>> or settling into an overly simplistic solution.
>
>A overly simplistic solution should generate error signals and lead to
>network growth, allowing more routes out of a minimum by increasing the
>dimension of the weight space.

 Solutions to most real-world problems generate some error signals. Deciding
which errors deserve to be tolerated and which should be solved by adding
nodes is handled more arbitrarily by current artificial learning rules than
it is by humans.

